<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The privacy manifest. Apple will not accept an App Store submission without
  one in the bundle, and it has to declare a reason for every "required reason
  API" the binary calls.

  Referenced as a resource by BOTH targets in ios-project.yml - the app and the
  widget extension. A manifest is not inherited from the host app, and the
  extension is the half that reads the App Group defaults.

  CHECK BEFORE SUBMITTING: Apple's "Describing use of required reason API"
  list grows, and a category added after this file was written is a rejection
  that names an API rather than this file. Re-read that page and confirm:
    - the two NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategory* names below are still spelled
      that way, and the reason codes CA92.1 and C617.1 still exist;
    - nothing new covers the Keychain, WidgetKit, WKWebView or
      URLSession/CFNetwork, which is where the rest of this app's system calls
      go;
    - whether tauri, wry, objc2 or security-framework now ship manifests of
      their own. A dependency's manifest is merged with this one at build
      time, so a category they declare does not need repeating here - but a
      category they call and nobody declares is still this app's rejection.
  Xcode's "Generate Privacy Report" on the archive is the check that reads the
  built binary rather than the source.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    False, and verified rather than assumed: grepping web/src and src-tauri/src
    for analytics, telemetry, beacon, and every vendor name that would imply
    one - sentry, segment, mixpanel, posthog, amplitude, datadog, bugsnag,
    crashlytics, gtag - finds nothing. The only hosts this app contacts are
    ones the reader configured: their GitHub, their S3 endpoint, and
    github.com avatars for the queue. None of that is tracking, and none of it
    is joined to data from another company.
  -->
  <key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
  <false/>
  <key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
  <array/>

  <!--
    Empty, deliberately. Apple counts data as "collected" when it is
    transmitted off the device in a way that lets the developer or a third
    party access it. Nothing here is:

    - The GitHub token and the S3 access key go to the iOS Keychain
      (src-tauri/src/keychain.rs, one service "dev.review", behind Face ID)
      and nowhere else. On platforms without one they go to IndexedDB instead
      (web/src/state/multi-event-store.js, `secret:<id>`), which is also local.
    - The token is sent to the reader's own GitHub when it authenticates a
      request to it, which is what a credential is for and not collection by
      this developer.
    - Drafts, reviews and queue contents live in the reader's own storage: the
      app's iCloud ubiquity container, their S3 bucket, or their GitHub
      repository. This app runs no server and has no account.

    Adding anything here - including a crash reporter or a "diagnostics"
    toggle - means a matching NSPrivacyCollectedDataType entry, and this
    comment stops being true.
  -->
  <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
  <array/>

  <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
  <array>
    <!--
      Earned by src-tauri/src/widget.rs: NSUserDefaults is opened with
      `initWithSuiteName("group.review.dev.app")` to write the queue count the
      widget shows, and ReviewerWidget.swift reads it back. CA92.1 is the
      app-group reason exactly - both bundles are members of that group, and
      the defaults carry nothing that is not already on screen in the app.
    -->
    <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</key>
    <array>
      <string>CA92.1</string>
    </array>

    <!--
      Earned by src-tauri/src/storage.rs: `walk` calls
      `fs::symlink_metadata` and `meta.modified()` on every file it lists, and
      `resolve` stats each path component while refusing anything that leaves
      the root. Rust's std::fs is stat(2), which is on Apple's list under this
      category.

      C617.1 is "files inside the app container, app group container, or
      CloudKit container". On iOS that is the only root these commands ever
      get: storage_pick_root is compiled out (see lib.rs), so the one root is
      the app's own iCloud ubiquity container from icloud.rs. Confident about
      the call and the container; the reason code is the part to confirm
      against Apple's current page.
    -->
    <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</key>
    <array>
      <string>C617.1</string>
    </array>
  </array>
</dict>
</plist>
